```

### ✅ NEW Approach: Pure Tailwind Classes

**Use Tailwind classes with design system colors:**

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Clean Tailwind classes
<div className="p-6 bg-card border border-border rounded-lg">Content</div>
```

### 🎯 When Custom CSS is Still Needed

Only create custom utility classes for cases where Tailwind can't help:

```css
/* app/globals.css */

/* Card utilities with proper borders */
.card {
  background-color: var(--card) !important;
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
}

.card-bordered {
  background-color: var(--card) !important;
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
}

.code-block {
  background-color: var(--background) !important;
  border-radius: var(--radius-md) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
}

/* Color swatch with border */
.color-swatch {
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
}
```

### Why `!important` is Needed

**Critical**: The `!important` flag is required to override Tailwind's default border styles. Without it, Tailwind's `border` utility class (which has `border-width: 0px` by default) will override your custom styles.

**Browser behavior:**

- Tailwind's `border` class sets `border-width: 0px` and requires additional classes like `border-2` to show
- Our utility classes explicitly set `border: 1px solid` but get overridden without `!important`
- Using `!important` ensures our Modus colors and borders always apply correctly

### Clean Usage in Components

```tsx
// ✅ CLEAN - Simple utility class
<div className="card-bordered p-6">
  Content
</div>

// ✅ CLEAN - Code block styling
<div className="code-block p-2 font-mono">
  <code>Sample code</code>
</div>

// ✅ CLEAN - Color swatch
<div className="color-swatch w-full h-24">
  Color preview
</div>
```

### When to Use Each Approach

**1. Utility Classes** (Preferred for common patterns):

```tsx
// ✅ For repeated patterns like cards, code blocks, swatches
<div className="card-bordered p-6">Content</div>
```

**2. Tailwind Classes** (For layout and spacing):

```tsx
// ✅ For layout, spacing, typography
<div className="flex items-center gap-4 p-6 rounded-lg">Content</div>
```

**3. Inline Styles** (Only for dynamic values):

```tsx
// ✅ Only when values are computed or dynamic
<div
  style={{ backgroundColor: isActive ? "var(--primary)" : "var(--background)" }}
>
  Content
</div>
```

### Creating New Utility Classes

When creating new utility classes, follow this pattern:

```css
/* globals.css */

.my-utility {
  /* Use Modus CSS variables */
  background-color: var(--card) !important;
  color: var(--foreground) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;

  /* Use design system tokens */
  border-radius: var(--radius-md) !important;
  padding: 1rem;
}
```

**Rules for utility classes:**

1. ✅ **Always use Modus CSS variables** for colors
2. ✅ **Add `!important`** to color and border properties
3. ✅ **Use design system tokens** for radius, spacing, etc.
4. ✅ **Name descriptively** (e.g., `.card-bordered`, `.code-block`)
5. ✅ **Keep it simple** - one purpose per utility class

### Common Utility Class Patterns

```css
/* Interactive cards */
.card-interactive {
  background-color: var(--card) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg) !important;
  transition: all 0.2s ease;
}

.card-interactive:hover {
  box-shadow: var(--elevation-sm);
  transform: translateY(-1px);
}

/* Status badges */
.badge-success {
  background-color: var(--success) !important;
  color: var(--success-foreground) !important;
  padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-badge) !important;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 500;
}

.badge-error {
  background-color: var(--destructive) !important;
  color: var(--destructive-foreground) !important;
  padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-badge) !important;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 500;
}

/* Form elements */
.input-field {
  background-color: var(--input-background) !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
  color: var(--foreground) !important;
  border-radius: var(--radius-md) !important;
  padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
}

.input-field:focus {
  outline: none;
  border-color: var(--ring) !important;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--ring);
}
```

### Testing Your Utility Classes

After creating utility classes, verify they work:

1. **Check the browser DevTools:**

```javascript
// In browser console
const el = document.querySelector(".card-bordered");
const styles = window.getComputedStyle(el);
console.log({
  borderWidth: styles.borderWidth, // Should be "1px"
  borderColor: styles.borderColor, // Should be Modus color
  backgroundColor: styles.backgroundColor,
});
```

2. **Visual verification:**

   - Open the page in browser
   - Check that borders are visible
   - Toggle themes to ensure colors adapt
   - Inspect element to verify styles are applied

3. **Hard reload if needed:**
   - CSS changes may require hard refresh (Cmd+Shift+R or Ctrl+Shift+R)
   - Clear cache if styles don't update
   - Restart dev server if necessary

### Migration Checklist

When converting from inline styles to utility classes:

- [ ] ✅ Create utility class in `globals.css` with Modus variables
- [ ] ✅ Add `!important` to color and border properties
- [ ] ✅ Test utility class in browser DevTools
- [ ] ✅ Replace inline styles with utility class
- [ ] ✅ Verify visual appearance matches
- [ ] ✅ Test with all 4 Modus themes
- [ ] ✅ Hard reload browser to clear CSS cache

### Benefits of This Approach

1. **✅ Clean Code**: No verbose inline style objects
2. **✅ DRY Principle**: Define once, use everywhere
3. **✅ Type Safety**: No TypeScript complexity for styles
4. **✅ Theme Compatible**: Automatic theme switching
5. **✅ Maintainable**: Change style in one place
6. **✅ Performance**: No runtime style calculations
7. **✅ Consistent**: Same styling patterns across app

